# Week 8: Single-Agent vs Multi-Agent

## Overview

This week compares a single tool-using agent against a 4-agent crew
(Researcher -> Analyst -> Writer -> Critic) on the same evaluation set.

Both systems share the same knowledge base and retrieval tool.

## Architecture

### Single Agent
Question -> retrieve -> generate -> answer

### Multi-Agent Crew
Question -> Researcher -> Analyst -> Writer -> Critic -> answer

## Comparison Table

| Strategy | Answer | Citation | Tokens | Latency (s) |
|----------|:------:|:--------:|:------:|:-----------:|
| Single Agent | 2/3 | 3/3 | 48 | 64.16 |
| Multi Agent | 1/3 | 3/3 | 551 | 363.55 |

## Failure Propagation

A failure is injected into the retrieval tool on the first call.

| Strategy | Detected | Recovered | Tokens | Latency (s) |
|----------|:--------:|:---------:|:------:|:-----------:|
| Single Agent | Yes | Yes | 30 | 32.86 |
| Multi Agent | Yes | No | 263 | 150.92 |

In the Multi-Agent crew, the failure propagates to every downstream agent.
Without context, the Writer hallucinates an answer instead of returning NOT FOUND.

## Verdict

The Multi-Agent crew is not worth the extra cost for this task:

- Lower quality (1/3 vs 2/3).
- ~11.5x more tokens.
- ~5.7x more latency.
- Failure propagates and causes hallucination.

Single Agent is the better choice for retrieval + generation tasks.

